%[text] %[text:anchor:T_ip0] # Estimate Interleaved Boost PFC Converter Parameters
%[text] This script sizes an interleaved boost power factor correction (PFC) power stage: a diode-bridge rectifier feeding two or more boost legs on evenly phase-shifted carriers.
%[text] From the AC input range, DC bus, rated power, leg count, switching frequency and allowed ripple, it returns the per-leg inductance. It also returns the DC-bus capacitance, the required device rating and the conduction mode.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ipov] ## Overview
%[text] The legs split the line current, so each carries $I\_{pk} /N${"editStyle":"visual"} and the conduction loss spreads over $N${"editStyle":"visual"} smaller devices. Their ripples partly cancel at the input node because the carriers are shifted by $T\_{sw} /N${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] Each leg is an ordinary boost stage. In continuous conduction its duty follows from volt-second balance, with modulation index $m=V\_{ac,pk} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $\\left|v\_{ac} \\left(t\\right)\\right|=\\left(1-d\\left(t\\right)\\right)V\_{out} \\;\\Longrightarrow \\;d\\left(t\\right)=1-m\\sin \\theta$
%[text] A boost converter cannot buck, so $m\<1${"editStyle":"visual"} is required at the highest line voltage. The script:
%[text] 1. Checks boost feasibility over the AC input range.
%[text] 2. Sizes the per-leg inductance $L${"editStyle":"visual"} from the worst-case per-leg ripple.
%[text] 3. Reports the ripple surviving at the input node, and where the cancellation is exact.
%[text] 4. Reports the continuous-conduction-mode (CCM) margin and the power below which conduction goes discontinuous.
%[text] 5. Sizes the DC-bus capacitance $C\_o${"editStyle":"visual"} and reports the required device rating, per leg for the boost devices and total for the bridge. \
%[text] Additional equations and definitions are provided in the [Annexure](internal:H_ipannex) at the end.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_iprate] ## Specify Converter Rating
%[text] Enter the interleaved boost PFC specifications. The current is highest at minimum line voltage. Feasibility, the inductance requirement and the CCM margin are worst at maximum.
%[text] The number of legs must match the plant block: it divides the current and sets the carrier phase shift and the number of gate signals.
interleavedSpec.minInputVoltage = simscape.Value(207,"V"); % Minimum AC input voltage (RMS)
interleavedSpec.nomInputVoltage = simscape.Value(230,"V"); % Nominal AC input voltage (RMS)
interleavedSpec.maxInputVoltage = simscape.Value(264,"V"); % Maximum AC input voltage (RMS)
interleavedSpec.lineFrequency   = simscape.Value(50,"Hz"); % AC line frequency

interleavedSpec.outputVoltage    = simscape.Value(400,"V");  % Regulated DC bus voltage
interleavedSpec.ratedPower       = simscape.Value(2000,"W"); % Rated (maximum) output power
interleavedSpec.numberOfLegs     = 2;                        % Interleaved boost legs, carriers shifted by Tsw/N
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ipsw] ## Specify Switching Frequency and Allowed Ripple
%[text] These two set the per-leg inductance. The ripple fraction is the peak-to-peak ripple in one leg as a fraction of that leg's peak current, at its worst point over the line cycle.
%[text] The DC-bus ripple fraction is peak-to-peak as a fraction of $V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} , at twice the line frequency. Interleaving does not reduce it.
interleavedSpec.switchingFrequency = simscape.Value(65e3,"Hz"); % Per-leg switching frequency
interleavedSpec.inductorCurrentRippleFraction = 0.30; % Peak-to-peak per-leg ripple as a fraction of peak leg current
interleavedSpec.outputVoltageRippleFraction = 0.05; % Peak-to-peak DC-bus ripple at 2*fLine (fraction of Vout)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_iphold] ## Specify Output Capacitor Hold-Up
%[text] On loss of mains the DC-bus capacitor holds the bus above a floor voltage for a ride-through time at rated power. Set the hold-up time to zero if ride-through is not required.
interleavedSpec.holdUpTime       = simscape.Value(16e-3,"s"); % Ride-through time at rated power after loss of mains
interleavedSpec.minHoldUpVoltage = simscape.Value(340,"V");  % Bus voltage floor during hold-up
%[text] The capacitance is the larger of the two requirements. Here the ripple requirement governs.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ipmarg] ## Specify Design Margins
%[text] The boost margin is how far the DC bus sits above the highest AC peak, and must be above 1. The device voltage margin sets the recommended device rating only.
%[text] Every leg blocks the DC bus, so interleaving splits the current but not the voltage. The bridge diodes block the line peak.
interleavedSpec.boostVoltageMargin  = 1.05; % Required ratio of Vout to the maximum AC peak
interleavedSpec.deviceVoltageMargin = 1.30; % Device voltage rating as a multiple of the blocking voltage
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ippara] ## Specify Parasitic Parameters
%[text] Parasitic resistances affect the device currents and the efficiency, so use datasheet values. The inductor resistance is per leg, and a leg carries $1/N${"editStyle":"visual"} of the line current.
%[text] The DC-bus capacitor starts at the regulated bus voltage, skipping the inrush and boost-up transient. A start-up study needs 0 V and an inrush-limiting element.
interleavedSpec.inductorResistance  = simscape.Value(0.03,"Ohm"); % Per-leg inductor series resistance
interleavedSpec.capacitorResistance = simscape.Value(0.01,"Ohm"); % DC-bus capacitor series resistance
interleavedSpec.initialCapacitorVoltage = interleavedSpec.outputVoltage; % DC-bus capacitor initial voltage
interleavedSpec.bridgeDiodeForwardVoltage = simscape.Value(0.7,"V"); % Per-diode drop, two in the path at all times
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ipest] ## Estimate Interleaved Boost PFC Parameters
%[text] Compute the per-leg inductance, the DC-bus capacitance and the ripple cancellation. `DisplayTableFlag` displays the design table below, with every current reported at its worst value over the AC input range. `SelectInterleavedBoostPFCComponents` gives the device voltage and current ratings.
%[text] `InterleavedBoostPFCDefaultInputParameters` computes the same values, so the model also opens without this script.
[designTable, interleavedDesign] = designInterleavedBoostPFCConverter(interleavedSpec, ... %[output:group:287e07f7] %[output:277e0664]
    "DisplayTableFlag", true); %[output:group:287e07f7] %[output:277e0664]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ipripple] ## Verify Current Ripple and Conduction Mode
%[text] Two plots over a line half cycle, at minimum, nominal and maximum AC input. The first overlays the per-leg ripple (solid) with the ripple surviving at the input node (dashed).
%[text] The per-leg ripple peaks where $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|=V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} . The input ripple depends on the fractional part of $Nd${"editStyle":"visual"} : cancellation is exact wherever $Nd${"editStyle":"visual"} is an integer, and worst halfway between, at $1/N${"editStyle":"visual"} of the per-leg worst case.
%[text] The envelope $i\_{L,\\textrm{leg}} \\pm \\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} gives the core saturation rating from its upper edge and the valley current from its lower edge, both per leg.
rippleData = plotInterleavedBoostPFCInductorCurrent(interleavedDesign); %[output:83685bbf]
%[text] The load below which the converter is in DCM over the whole AC half cycle is set at the input voltage peak. The load above which it is in CCM over the whole cycle is set at the zero crossing, where the shaped leg current is smallest. Both loads are totals, so each carries the leg count. In the figure, the converter is in CCM over the entire cycle in the green region and in DCM over the entire cycle in the red region. Between the two it is discontinuous only around the zero crossings.
plotInterleavedBoostPFCCCMBoundary(interleavedDesign); %[output:41a8d553]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ipctrl] ## Specify Controller Parameters
%[text] Tune the average-current-mode controller against the sized power stage. An outer voltage PI produces a power command. Multiplied by the per-unit rectified line voltage, that command is the per-unit current reference, and one shared current loop tracks it with a common duty.
%[text] Inductor tolerance and device drops make the legs drift apart at identical duties. A differential balance loop trims the difference without changing the sum.
%[text] The voltage loop is held near 3 Hz, which keeps the 100 Hz bus ripple out of the current reference.
[controllerTable, interleavedController] = designInterleavedBoostPFCController(interleavedDesign, ... %[output:group:58183636] %[output:8fa821f5]
    PhaseMargin = 60, ...              % Target phase margin, all loops (deg) %[output:8fa821f5]
    CurrentCrossoverRatio = 20, ...    % Current-loop crossover as Fsw/ratio %[output:8fa821f5]
    VoltageCrossoverFrequency = 3, ... % Voltage-loop crossover (Hz) %[output:8fa821f5]
    BalanceCrossoverRatio = 100, ...   % Balance-loop crossover as Fsw/ratio, slower than the current loop %[output:8fa821f5]
    DutyBalanceMax = 0.1, ...          % Balance-loop duty authority, caps how far the legs can be trimmed apart %[output:8fa821f5]
    IrefPeakMaxPu = 1.3, ...           % Peak current-reference limit (pu of the rated line-current peak) %[output:8fa821f5]
    LoadFraction = 1, ...              % Power command as a fraction of rated power %[output:8fa821f5]
    ModulatorGuardTime = 100e-9, ...   % Modulator OFF window at both ends of the carrier period (s) %[output:8fa821f5]
    PwmStepsPerPeriod = 20, ...       % Fixed-step modulator resolution per carrier period %[output:8fa821f5]
    GateVoltageOn = 15, GateVoltageOff = 0, ... % Gate drive levels (V) %[output:8fa821f5]
    DisplayTableFlag = true); %[output:group:58183636] %[output:8fa821f5]
%[text] The quantities the controller shares with the plant - AC input, DC bus, rated power, switching frequency, leg count - come from `interleavedDesign`.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ipannex] ## Annexure: Equations and Definitions
%[text] **Boost feasibility and duty.** With $V\_{pk} =\\sqrt{2}V\_{rms}${"editStyle":"visual"} and $m=V\_{pk} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} , the bus must satisfy $V\_{out} \\ge \\textrm{boostVoltageMargin}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}V\_{ac,\\max }${"editStyle":"visual"} , and the CCM duty of each leg and its minimum are:
%[text] $d\\left(\\theta \\right)=1-m\\sin \\theta \\;,\\quad d\_{\\min } =1-m\\;\\textrm{at}\\;\\theta =\\pi /2$
%[text] **Line and leg currents (unity power factor).** The bridge carries the whole line current; the legs share it equally:
%[text] $I\_{rms} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{V\_{rms} }\\;,\\quad I\_{pk} =\\sqrt{2}I\_{rms} \\;,\\quad i\_{L,\\textrm{leg}} \\left(\\theta \\right)=\\frac{I\_{pk} }{N}\\sin \\theta$
%[text] **Per-leg ripple and inductance.** Each leg sees the full bus, so its ripple is the ordinary boost result, quadratic in $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|${"editStyle":"visual"} . The inductance follows from the worst case over the AC input range:
%[text] $\\Delta I\_{L,\\max } =\\frac{V\_{out} }{4Lf\_{sw} }\\;,\\quad L=\\max\_{V\_{ac} } \\frac{V\_{out} }{4f\_{sw} r\_i I\_{pk} /N}$
%[text] **Interleaving cancellation.** Summing $N${"editStyle":"visual"} triangular leg currents shifted by $T\_{sw} /N${"editStyle":"visual"} gives an input ripple that depends only on the fractional part $f=\\textrm{frac}\\left(Nd\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $\\Delta I\_{in} =\\frac{V\_{out} f\\left(1-f\\right)}{NLf\_{sw} }\\;\\Longrightarrow \\;\\Delta I\_{in,\\max } =\\frac{V\_{out} }{4NLf\_{sw} }=\\frac{\\Delta I\_{L,\\max } }{N}$
%[text] The cancellation is therefore exact at every duty where $Nd${"editStyle":"visual"} is an integer, and the worst surviving ripple is $1/N${"editStyle":"visual"} of the per-leg worst case. Its frequency is $Nf\_{sw}${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] **Continuous conduction.** Requiring $i\_L \>\\Delta I\_L /2${"editStyle":"visual"} in a leg, the $\\sin \\theta${"editStyle":"visual"} cancels and leaves $I\_{pk} /N\>V\_{pk} \\left(1-m\\sin \\theta \\right)/\\left(2Lf\_{sw} \\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} . The surviving factor is the duty, so it is largest at the line zero crossing and smallest at the line peak. The zero crossing is therefore the tightest point, and it sets the load at which conduction first goes discontinuous. The power threshold carries the factor $N${"editStyle":"visual"} because it is a total, not a per-leg, power:
%[text] $L\_{CCM} =\\frac{V\_{pk} }{2f\_{sw} I\_{pk} /N}\\;,\\quad P\_{CCM} =\\frac{NV\_{rms}^2 }{2f\_{sw} L}$
%[text] The line peak sets the other end of the same criterion, below which conduction is discontinuous over the whole cycle. Its duty is $1-m${"editStyle":"visual"} , so that load is lower by the same factor:
%[text] $P\_{DCM} =\\left(1-m\\right)\\frac{NV\_{rms}^2 }{2f\_{sw} L}=\\left(1-m\\right)P\_{CCM}$
%[text] **The ripple fraction and the CCM range are one degree of freedom.** Substituting the sized $L${"editStyle":"visual"} at the maximum line voltage, which sets both the inductance and the smallest margin, collapses the two CCM results to reciprocal identities containing neither $f\_{sw}${"editStyle":"visual"} , $L${"editStyle":"visual"} , $N${"editStyle":"visual"} nor the power level:
%[text] $\\frac{L}{L\_{CCM} }=\\frac{1}{2r\_i m}\\;,\\quad \\frac{P\_{CCM} }{P\_{rated} }=2r\_i m\\;\\Longrightarrow \\;\\textrm{full}-\\textrm{cycle}\\;\\textrm{CCM}\\;\\textrm{needs}\\;r\_i \<\\frac{1}{2m}$
%[text] That identity is written in terms of $r\_i${"editStyle":"visual"} , which is defined at the ripple maximum $\\left|v\_{ac} \\right|=V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} , so it holds only while the line peak reaches that point. The margin itself is computed from $L/L\_{CCM}${"editStyle":"visual"} directly, which is exact at every AC input, including one whose peak stays below $V\_{out} /2${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] **DC-bus capacitance.** A single-phase PFC draws $P\_{in} \\left(1-\\cos 2\\omega\_{line} t\\right)${"editStyle":"visual"} against a constant load, so the capacitor absorbs the whole pulsating term regardless of the number of legs. With the hold-up requirement from the energy balance, the capacitance is the larger of:
%[text] $C\_{o,\\textrm{ripple}} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{\\omega\_{line} r\_v V\_{out}^2 }\\;,\\quad C\_{o,\\textrm{holdup}} =\\frac{2P\_{out} t\_{\\textrm{hold}} }{V\_{out}^2 -V\_{\\min }^2 }\\;,\\quad I\_{Co,rms} =\\frac{P\_{out} }{\\sqrt{2}V\_{out} }$
%[text] **Leg conduction currents.** The single-leg closed forms apply to each leg with $I\_{pk} /N${"editStyle":"visual"} in place of $I\_{pk}${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $I\_{Q,rms} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{N}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{4m}{3\\pi }}\\;,\\quad I\_{D,rms} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{N}\\sqrt{\\frac{4m}{3\\pi }}\\;,\\quad I\_{D,avg} =\\frac{I\_{pk} m}{2N}$
%[text] Two exact identities make these self-checking: $I\_{Q,rms}^2 +I\_{D,rms}^2 =I\_{L,rms}^2${"editStyle":"visual"} per leg, because the duty splits one current, and $NI\_{D,avg} =P\_{in} /V\_{out}${"editStyle":"visual"} , because the boost diodes together feed the bus.
%[text] **Bridge diodes.** The bridge sits before the split, so it carries the full line current and gains nothing from interleaving. Each diode conducts one half cycle and blocks the line peak, never the bus:
%[text] $I\_{rect,avg} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{\\pi }\\;,\\quad I\_{rect,rms} =\\frac{I\_{pk} }{2}\\;,\\quad P\_{\\textrm{bridge}} =2V\_f \\frac{2I\_{pk} }{\\pi }$
%[text] **Not sized here.** The EMI filter, inrush limiting, reverse recovery and the thermal design are out of scope. So is the inductor tolerance the balance loop exists to absorb. A boost leg is one switch and one diode, so there is no dead time to derive; the modulator's minimum on-time guard is a hardware limit taken as given.
%[text] ### Variable Definitions
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_{rms} ,V\_{pk} -\\textrm{AC}\\;\\textrm{input}\\;\\textrm{voltage},\\;\\textrm{RMS}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{peak}\\\\\nV\_{out} -\\textrm{Regulated}\\;\\textrm{DC}\\;\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\\\\nN-\\textrm{Number}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{interleaved}\\;\\textrm{legs}\\;\\left(\\textrm{carriers}\\;\\textrm{shifted}\\;\\textrm{by}\\;T\_{sw} /N\\right)\\\\\nm=V\_{pk} /V\_{out} -\\textrm{Modulation}\\;\\textrm{index}\\;\\left(m\<1\\;\\textrm{required}\\right)\\\\\nd\\left(\\theta \\right)-\\textrm{CCM}\\;\\textrm{duty}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{each}\\;\\textrm{leg};\\;f=\\textrm{frac}\\left(Nd\\right)\\\\\n\\theta =\\omega\_{line} t-\\textrm{Line}\\;\\textrm{phase};\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{half}\\;\\textrm{cycle}\\;\\textrm{is}\\;\\theta \\in \\left\\lbrack 0,\\pi \\right\\rbrack \\\\\nL-\\textrm{Per}-\\textrm{leg}\\;\\textrm{boost}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\;\\left(\\textrm{plant}\\;\\textrm{LBoost}\\right)\\\\\nC\_o -\\textrm{DC}-\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\;\\left(\\textrm{plant}\\;\\textrm{CBoost}\\right)\\\\\nf\_{sw} -\\textrm{Per}-\\textrm{leg}\\;\\textrm{switching}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\nf\_{line} ,\\omega\_{line} -\\textrm{Line}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{its}\\;\\textrm{angular}\\;\\textrm{value}\\\\\nI\_{pk} ,I\_{rms} -\\textrm{Peak}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{RMS}\\;\\textrm{total}\\;\\textrm{line}\\;\\textrm{current}\\\\\nr\_i -\\textrm{Peak}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{PER}-\\textrm{LEG}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{as}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;I\_{pk} /N\\\\\nr\_v -\\textrm{Peak}-\\textrm{to}-\\textrm{peak}\\;\\textrm{DC}-\\textrm{bus}\\;\\textrm{ripple}\\;\\textrm{as}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;V\_{out} \\\\\nV\_f -\\textrm{Bridge}\\;\\textrm{diode}\\;\\textrm{forward}\\;\\textrm{voltage}\\;\\left(\\textrm{two}\\;\\textrm{in}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{path}\\right)\n\\end{array}$
%%
